= Collective intelligence — Simple =

'''Collective intelligence''' is the ability of a group or coordinated system to solve problems, make judgments, or produce useful knowledge using contributions from several participants.

Groups can become more capable in different ways.

One group might average many independent estimates. Another might divide a difficult problem among specialists. A third might discuss evidence and correct mistakes together.

== Wisdom of crowds ==

A group can sometimes estimate something more accurately than most of its individual members.

This works best when people bring partly independent information. One person's error can cancel another person's error.

But independence matters.

If everyone copies the same incorrect source, averaging hundreds of answers does not create hundreds of independent observations.

Experiments have also shown that social influence can reduce diversity of estimates and weaken crowd accuracy.<ref name="lorenz">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21576485/ Lorenz, J. et al. (2011), “How social influence can undermine the wisdom of crowd effect.”]</ref>

== Groups that work together ==

Interactive teams can do more than average answers.

They can:

* divide tasks;
* combine different expertise;
* challenge mistakes;
* build shared plans;
* create solutions that no member had at the beginning.

Interaction can also cause problems such as groupthink, dominance, conformity, or communication overload.

== Measuring collective intelligence ==

Anita Woolley and colleagues reported that small groups' performance across different tasks could be summarized by a common collective-intelligence factor.<ref name="woolley">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20929725/ Woolley, A. W. et al. (2010), “Evidence for a collective intelligence factor in the performance of human groups.”]</ref>

A later analysis by Riedl and colleagues combined 22 studies with 5,279 individuals in 1,356 groups and found evidence that collective-intelligence performance generalized across several tasks.<ref name="riedl">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8166150/ Riedl, C. et al. (2021), “Quantifying collective intelligence in human groups.”]</ref>

These studies measure group performance. They do not prove that a group has a single consciousness or mind.

== What helps? ==

Collective performance can depend on:

* useful individual skills;
* different information sources;
* social sensitivity;
* fair communication;
* good task division;
* clear goals;
* effective coordination.

Diversity is useful only when different perspectives actually enter the process.

== Crowdsourcing ==

Online systems can collect contributions from thousands or millions of people.

Examples include collaborative encyclopedias, citizen-science projects, open-source software, and data-labeling projects.

A large crowd is not automatically intelligent. The system still needs good tasks, quality control, coordination, and correction.

== Humans and AI ==

People can also work with AI systems as part of a collective.

AI may help with search, parallel analysis, or drafting. But several agents using the same model may repeat the same error.

Human–AI systems should therefore be tested against human-only and AI-only baselines.

